      <title>Physics Reference</title>
      <link>https://0ffy.github.io/posts/2025-03-11-physics/</link>
      <pubDate>Tue, 11 Mar 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://0ffy.github.io/posts/2025-03-11-physics/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;This is a reference of physics mostly taken from the textbook &amp;ldquo;&lt;em&gt;University physics with modern physics&lt;/em&gt;&amp;rdquo;. 







&lt;span class=&#34;hugo-cite-intext&#34;
        itemprop=&#34;citation&#34;&gt;(&lt;span class=&#34;hugo-cite-group&#34;&gt;

          &lt;a href=&#34;#uniphysics-bibref&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;visually-hidden&#34;&gt;Citation: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span itemprop=&#34;author&#34; itemscope itemtype=&#34;https://schema.org/Person&#34;&gt;&lt;meta itemprop=&#34;givenName&#34; content=&#34;Hugh D&#34;&gt;&lt;span itemprop=&#34;familyName&#34;&gt;Young&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;,&amp;#32;&lt;span itemprop=&#34;author&#34; itemscope itemtype=&#34;https://schema.org/Person&#34;&gt;&lt;meta itemprop=&#34;givenName&#34; content=&#34;Roger A&#34;&gt;&lt;span itemprop=&#34;familyName&#34;&gt;Freedman&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
                  &lt;em&gt;&amp;amp; al.&lt;/em&gt;,&amp;#32;&lt;span itemprop=&#34;datePublished&#34;&gt;2011&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;hugo-cite-citation&#34;&gt; 










&lt;span itemscope 
      itemtype=&#34;https://schema.org/Book&#34;
      data-type=&#34;book&#34;&gt;&lt;span itemprop=&#34;author&#34; itemscope itemtype=&#34;https://schema.org/Person&#34;&gt;&lt;span itemprop=&#34;familyName&#34;&gt;Young&lt;/span&gt;,&amp;#32;
    &lt;meta itemprop=&#34;givenName&#34; content=&#34;Hugh D&#34; /&gt;
    H.&lt;/span&gt;,&amp;#32;
  &lt;span itemprop=&#34;author&#34; itemscope itemtype=&#34;https://schema.org/Person&#34;&gt;&lt;span itemprop=&#34;familyName&#34;&gt;Freedman&lt;/span&gt;,&amp;#32;
    &lt;meta itemprop=&#34;givenName&#34; content=&#34;Roger A&#34; /&gt;
    R.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;#32;&amp;amp;&amp;#32;&lt;span itemprop=&#34;author&#34; itemscope itemtype=&#34;https://schema.org/Person&#34;&gt;&lt;span itemprop=&#34;familyName&#34;&gt;Ford&lt;/span&gt;,&amp;#32;
    &lt;meta itemprop=&#34;givenName&#34; content=&#34;A Lewis&#34; /&gt;
    A.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;#32;
    (&lt;span itemprop=&#34;datePublished&#34;&gt;2011&lt;/span&gt;).
  &amp;#32;&lt;span itemprop=&#34;name&#34;&gt;
    &lt;i&gt;University physics with modern physics 13th edition&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/span&gt;.
  &amp;#32;
  &lt;span itemprop=&#34;publisher&#34;
             itemtype=&#34;http://schema.org/Organization&#34;
             itemscope=&#34;&#34;&gt;
    &lt;span itemprop=&#34;name&#34;&gt;Addison-Wesley Reading, MA&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;




&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;mechanics&#34;&gt;Mechanics&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&#34;newtons-laws&#34;&gt;Newton&amp;rsquo;s laws&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;(Inertia): an object will remain at rest or in uniform motion in a straight line unless acted upon by an external force.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;($F=ma$) acceleration is caused by force.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;(Reaction) for every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&#34;energies&#34;&gt;Energies&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Work is defined as the dot product of force and displacement: $W = \vec{F}\cdot\vec{s}$. For a constant force acting in the direction of a straight line, $W=Fs$. For varying forces $F=F(x)$ on a straight line, work is the integral of $F$ w.r.t. $x$:
$$
W = \int_{x_1}^{x_2}F(x)dx
$$
The SI unit of work is joule (J), $1 J = 1 N\cdot m$. From
$$
a=\frac{dv}{dt}=\frac{dv}{dx}\frac{dx}{dt}=v\cdot\frac{dv}{dx},
$$
the kinetic energy is defined as
$$
K = \int Fdx = \int madx = m\int v\frac{dv}{dx}dx = m\int vdv = \frac{1}{2}mv^2
$$
and the work-energy theorem is
$$
W_{\text{tot}} = K_2 - K_1 = \Delta K.
$$&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Black-Scholes Model</title>
      <link>https://0ffy.github.io/posts/2024-11-26-black-scholes/</link>
      <pubDate>Tue, 26 Nov 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://0ffy.github.io/posts/2024-11-26-black-scholes/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;basics&#34;&gt;Basics&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An option is a financial contract that gives you the right (but not obligation) to buy or sell usually 100 shares of the underlying asset at a strike price $X$ at or before some specific date, usually Fridays. The price of an option is quoted on a &lt;em&gt;per share&lt;/em&gt; basis, for example 5.5 USD. To buy the option, you have to pay 5.5 x 100 = 550 USD. If the option later rises to 6.0 USD, you can sell the option and get a profit of (6-5.5) x 100 = 50 USD. For convenience, in this post we will omit the multiplier and hold our discussions on a per share basis.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
