    <item>
      <title>NLP Models</title>
      <link>https://0ffy.github.io/posts/2022-03-01-nlp/</link>
      <pubDate>Tue, 01 Mar 2022 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://0ffy.github.io/posts/2022-03-01-nlp/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;A &lt;em&gt;language model&lt;/em&gt; is a model that assigns probabilities to words and sentences. The probabilities should reflect word statistics in the training data. Probabilities can be very useful for many applications, e.g. for grammar correction,
$$
\mathbb{P}(\text{This }\textit{means}\text{ in particular}) &amp;gt; \mathbb{P}(\text{This }\textit{mean}\text{  in particular}).
$$&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For model evaluation, &lt;strong&gt;extrinsic evaluation&lt;/strong&gt; refers to performance measurements in real-world tasks like spelling correction or speech recognition. &lt;strong&gt;Intrinsic evaluation&lt;/strong&gt; refers to calculating probabilities on some test set, to see which model outputs larger numbers. The common metric is the &lt;strong&gt;perplexity&lt;/strong&gt;. For a test set $W=w_1\cdots w_N$, it computes
$$
PP(w) = \sqrt[N]{\frac{1}{\mathbb{P}(w_1\cdots w_N)}}.
$$&lt;/p&gt;</description>
